The working tree’s runtime guard is a strict membership check on both `load()` and `initialize()`, and the shared Next/Nuxt contract now persists the bad canonical snapshot and retries through `load()`.

### Finding 1 (closed): `seeded: false` after a failed initialize cannot mint on `load()`

`prepareFreshState(..., true)` is used on **both** snapshot sources:

```198:225:packages/@emulators/core/src/adapter-runtime.ts
              if (snapshot.seeded === false) return { ...(await prepareFreshState(generatedSecrets, true)), snapshot };
              return { snapshot, seeds: new Map(), generatedSecrets };
            } catch {
              if (needsDurableGeneratedIdentity) {
                throw new Error("Cannot restore persisted emulator state without replacing generated identities");
              }
            }
          }
        }
        const fresh = await prepareFreshState();
        // ...
        if (canonical.seeded !== false) return { snapshot: canonical, seeds: new Map(), generatedSecrets };
        return { ...(await prepareFreshState(generatedSecrets, true)), snapshot: canonical };
```

A failed initialize still clears `preparationPromise` (`adapter-runtime.ts:228-230`). Retry does not call `initialize()` again once persistence has a body: `getPreparation()` takes the `if (raw)` branch and the `seeded === false` arm above.

That throw cannot mint on the GitHub App path. Strict failure is inside the `load()` `try`, but `needsDurableGeneratedIdentity` is true for this seed (`private_key === undefined` plus `needsGeneratedSecrets`), so the catch rethrows instead of falling through to `prepareFreshState()` at line 207. `generatedSecrets()` never reaches `initApps` / `enqueueSave`.

The shared contract now actually stores the bad snapshot, then builds a **second** handler (the load/isolate retry):

```172:182:tests/contracts/github-app-identity.js
    it("refuses a persisted canonical snapshot that omits the configured identity", async () => {
      const persistence = memory();
      const initialize = persistence.initialize;
      persistence.initialize = async (data) => {
        const canonical = JSON.parse(data);
        canonical.generatedSecrets[0].service = "other";
        return initialize(JSON.stringify(canonical));
      };
      for (let attempt = 0; attempt < 2; attempt++)
        await expect(create(persistence).generatedSecrets()).rejects.toThrow(/generated identities/);
    });
```

Attempt 0: `load()` is null, `initialize()` writes `{ seeded: false, service: "other" }`, strict throws. Attempt 1: `memory().value` is set, so the new handler only `load()`s. Same rejection.

### Finding 2 (closed): list length cannot hide a newly minted identity

The length check is gone. Strict mode rejects any **prepared** `(service, kind, id, value)` that is not already in the canonical list:

```68:71:packages/@emulators/core/src/adapter-runtime.ts
const isNonEmptyString = (value: unknown): value is string => typeof value === "string" && value.length > 0;
const isGeneratedSecret = (value: unknown): value is GeneratedSecret =>
  isRecord(value) && [value.service, value.kind, value.id, value.label, value.value].every(isNonEmptyString);
const secretIdentity = ({ service, kind, id, value }: GeneratedSecret) => JSON.stringify([service, kind, id, value]);
```

```179:182:packages/@emulators/core/src/adapter-runtime.ts
    const restoredIdentities = new Set(restoredSecrets.map(secretIdentity));
    if (strict && generatedSecrets.some((secret) => !restoredIdentities.has(secretIdentity(secret)))) {
      throw new Error("Cannot initialize emulator state without replacing generated identities");
    }
```

Service filtering is still `secret.service === name` (`adapter-runtime.ts:171-176`). A row that does not belong to the configured service is dropped, GitHub mints, and the new identity is not in `restoredIdentities`.

| Bypass | What happens now |
|---|---|
| **Wrong-service** | Dropped by the filter, GitHub mints, strict throws. Covered by the contract test (`service = "other"`). |
| **Padded extras** | Extra canonical rows are allowed (they are not new prepared identities). A missing configured app still mints, and that new tuple fails membership even if the list length is unchanged. |
| **Duplicates** | `prepareSeed` copies duplicates and does not generate when the id already has a nonempty value. No new identity. Duplicates of the wrong id still leave the real app missing, so mint + strict throw. |
| **Empty metadata** | `undefined` / `[]` freeze to `[]`, GitHub mints, strict throws. `value: ""` (and empty `service`/`kind`/`id`/`label`) fail `isGeneratedSecret`, so `parseSnapshot` throws. On `load()` that is remapped to the restore error when durable identity is required; on `initialize()` it is `Persistence initialize() returned an invalid snapshot`. Neither path seeds or saves a replacement key. |

The old empty-value hole in `prepareSeed` (`if (!privateKey) return app` plus `generatedIds.has(id) continue` at `packages/@emulators/github/src/index.ts:191-203`) is unreachable from adapter snapshots, because empty `value` never survives parse.

### Shared Next / Nuxt contract

Both adapters only bind `githubAppIdentityContract` and `runtime.generatedSecrets`:

- `packages/@emulators/adapter-next/src/__tests__/github-app-identity.test.ts:15`
- `packages/@emulators/adapter-nuxt/src/__tests__/github-app-identity.test.ts:15`
- Next: sibling of `GET`/`POST` (`adapter-next/src/index.ts:35`)
- Nuxt: property on the handler (`adapter-nuxt/src/index.ts:102`)

There is no second identity path around the core runtime.

### Regression scan

No new mint, restore, or retry hole showed up in the fix:

- Happy path still restores the canonical PEM: second `prepareFreshState` uses `restoredKeys`, so it does not generate (`github/src/index.ts:191-193`).
- Concurrent `initialize()` create-or-read, fail-closed first save, `seeded: true` re-read after `generatedSecrets()`, explicit keys staying off the generated list, and snapshots without `generatedSecrets` still authenticating from the App row are unchanged.
- `load()` remapping a strict throw to the restore message is still fail-closed for this seed. It does not resume at line 207.

Residual (not actionable): the contract asserts two throws, not every pad/duplicate/empty fixture, and `prepareSeed` still has the `continue`. That `continue` cannot run on secrets that passed `isGeneratedSecret`.
